course: dictionary
lesson: 11847
title: Blub and crying words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Blub is an informal British word that means to cry noisily, often in a babyish way. «Stop blubbing, it was only a scraped knee.» The speaker wants the child to stop crying loudly over a small hurt, so blubbing means crying noisily.
explain: Blub is a short form of blubber. The clues in a sentence tell you its meaning: noise, tears, and a feeling that the crying is a bit silly. «Tom blubbed when he dropped his ice cream» shows a child crying loudly over something small.
explain: Blub is a verb, so its form changes: blubs, blubbed, blubbing. «She blubbed all the way home.» Try the meaning in the sentence: she cried loudly all the way home. It still makes sense, so you have found the right meaning.
fact: Blub is a verb: blub, blubs, blubbed, blubbing.
fact: «Blub» means to cry noisily, often in a babyish way.
fact: Blub is informal and is used mostly in the UK.
fact: Blub is a short form of blubber.
fact: People often say blub a little unkindly, so think about how it might make someone feel before you use it.
exercise: «Stop blubbing, it was only a scraped knee.» What does blubbing mean? | Sleeping deeply | Laughing loudly | *Crying noisily | Running fast
exercise: «Tom blubbed when he dropped his ice cream.» What is Tom doing? | Counting his coins | *Crying loudly over something small | Eating quickly | Shouting at a friend
exercise: Which word is the verb form for the past of blub? | blubs | *blubbed | blub-ish | blubbing
test: «The little girl blubbed all the way home.» What does blubbed mean here? | Sang happily | Walked slowly | *Cried noisily | Ran into the house
test: Which sentence uses blub in the right form? | She blubs every hour of the day, now. | *She is blubbing again. | She blub yesterday. | Blubbing she is.
exam: Jamal says, «Don't blub, it's only a game.» What does blub mean, and how do you know? | *To cry noisily; the words "don't" and "only a game" show he is telling someone to stop crying | To run fast; the word "game" means a race | To laugh loudly; the word "game" shows it is fun | To sleep; the word "only" shows someone is tired
